WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.740
A fresh catalog of nearly 3,000 supernovae is already testing long held ideas about what drives the universe's expansion. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 192. Researchers compiled the largest catalog yet of exploding white dwarf stars, totaling close to 3,000 events. The sample supplies new distance measurements that refine estimates of how fast the universe expands. Early analysis shows the data may require adjustments to some

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:59.080
current dark energy descriptions. The most comprehensive catalog of exploding white dwarf stars ever assembled has revealed new clues about dark energy. Each added event, Titans constraints on models of cosmic acceleration. The catalog's uniform selection criteria reduce systematic uncertainties that have limited previous samples. This scale of data collection marks a shift towards statistical studies of Type 1A supernovae rather than individual case examinations.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:26.920
From the largest scale questions about the universe's expansion, we turn to how nations are coordinating on our nearest celestial neighbor. The move positions the country among nations coordinating on moon missions and resource use. Officials highlighted plans to develop national capabilities in lunar science and technology through international partnerships. This agreement aligns Turkey A with other signatories in promoting peaceful and cooperative space activities.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:54.120
The focus includes building expertise for future lunar surface operations and scientific investigations. While nations align on lunar goals, nature continues to surprise us closer to home at the Milky Way's center. Young stars orbiting the Milky Way's central black hole appear to have lost their binary partners at higher rates than expected. The black holes gravitational influence is thought to both shape their orbits and disrupt paired systems.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:02:12.120
The study was published in astronomy and astrophysics on August 11th. Observations indicate that the intense tidal forces near the black hole can strip companions from young stellar systems. This process leaves a population of apparently single stars in orbits that would normally favor binaries.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:28.160
The Milky Way's central black hole, Sagittarius A-Star, packs 4 million solar masses into a region smaller than our solar system, yet young stars orbit it at distances comparable to the outer planets at speeds exceeding 1000 kilometers per second.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:36.340
Imagine riding alongside one of these stars as the black holes gravity stretches and tugs at any companion star traveling nearby.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:51.460
The differential pull can exceed the companion's own gravitational binding energy within a single orbit. Observations published August 11th show that the fraction of binaries among these young stars is far lower than in quieter regions of the galaxy.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:03:01.080
Stars at roughly 0.1 light year from the black hole experience tidal forces strong enough to unbind solar mass companions in under a million years.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:13.640
We can map the resulting single star orbits with precision, but nobody yet knows whether the missing companions were ejected, swallowed, or transformed into something we have not yet detected in the dense stellar environment.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:22.580
From extreme gravitational environments, we shift to an engineering solution that could keep satellites aloft using nothing but the thin air around them.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:31.320
A new engine concept developed in a University of Stuttgart PhD thesis uses air molecules in very low Earth orbit as propellant.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:46.100
Satellites between 100 and 450 kilometers altitude would no longer need stored xenon or similar gases. The design aims to let spacecraft maintain orbit indefinitely while drag naturally removes debris at end of life.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:53.720
Francesco Romano's work demonstrates ionization and acceleration of residual nitrogen and oxygen to produce thrust.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:04:05.500
This removes the mass penalty of carrying dedicated fuel for station keeping. While new tools refine what we see from orbit, microlensing surveys keep adding to the catalog of distant worlds.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:18.480
KMT 2025 BLG 0026LB is a gas giant that orbits its star once every 1.4 years at a distance of 0.96 astronomical units.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:30.200
Its discovery was announced in 2026 through microlensing observations. The planet's mass of 2.13 Jupiter's places it among the larger examples found by this method.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:42.740
KMT 2025 BLG 0030LB is a 1.15 Jupiter mass planet that circles its host star at 2.35 astronomical units.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:54.160
The 2026 announcement came from the same microlensing survey program. The longer orbital period reflects the greater separation from its star compared with closer in giants.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:05:07.620
Ogle 2016 BLG 0261LB has a mass of 0.21 Jupiter's and completes an orbit every 2.3 years at 0.72 astronomical units.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:18.100
Its parameters were published in 2026. The relatively low mass for a gas giant makes it an interesting test case for formation models at sub-saturn scales.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:32.120
HD 715B is a 0.635 Jupiter mass planet that circles an F-type star at 0.336 AU. Discovery details were released in 2026.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:36.680
The short period places the planet well inside the snow line of its host.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:49.800
HD 278,555B is a 0.84 Jupiter mass world that orbits at 0.1985 AU around an F-type star.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:06:00.380
The 2026 announcement adds another close in giant to the catalog. The orbital distance corresponds to roughly half the mercury sun separation in our system.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:06.680
From fresh planetary discoveries, we stepped back to a milestone that first proved we could reach another world.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:14.940
The Soviet spacecraft Luna 2 launched on September 12th and impacted the lunar surface after roughly 30 hours of flight.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:22.600
It struck at about 1.9 miles per second, marking the first human-made object to reach another celestial body.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:29.760
The mission followed Sputnik I by less than two years and demonstrated precise interplanetary navigation.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:35.760
Impact occurred on the lunar surface at a speed that ensured the spacecraft did not survive intact.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:40.800
From that early achievement, we closed with a few striking images captured this week.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:49.860
Photographer Osama Fathi captured per seed meteors crossing long star trails in Egypt's western desert on August 13 to 14.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:53.960
The image highlights the annual shower against a desert horizon.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:07:04.860
I see 1396 lies about 2,400 light years away in Sepheus and contains a 20 light year dark pillar where new stars are forming.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:11.380
The entire region spans nearly three degrees of sky, equivalent to six full moons side by side.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:21.760
SN2025RBS and SN2026AAIV were both recorded in the same galaxy using identical equipment.

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:34.720
Both observations employed a 4-inch refractor, Z00107FF corrector, and QHY268M mono-camera with opt-along LRGB filters.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:42.820
The emission nebula IC-1805 sits roughly 7,500 light years away in Cassiopeia.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:51.420
A total of 25 hours and 35 minutes of exposure through a red cat 51 telescope produced the final image in the Hubble palette.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:08:04.120
Looking back over the past week, the biggest threads have been the steady drumbeat of results from fascinating frontiers, fresh NASA catalog releases, and coordinated moves by ESA and Jaxa partners.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:13.460
Those threads show both the expanding reach of statistical astronomy and the growing number of agencies turning raw observations into shared infrastructure.

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:20.340
It has been a week of bigger samples and tighter international coordination, setting the stage for what comes next.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:29.560
Before we go, keep an eye on follow-up observations from the new Supernova catalog that could clarify the hints of tension with earlier dark energy models.

